 3481|     42|{
 3482|       | /*	Arguments ndigits, decpt, sign are similar to those
 3483|       |	of ecvt and fcvt; trailing zeros are suppressed from
 3484|       |	the returned string.  If not null, *rve is set to point
 3485|       |	to the end of the return value.  If d is +-Infinity or NaN,
 3486|       |	then *decpt is set to 9999.
 3487|       |
 3488|       |	mode:
 3489|       |		0 ==> shortest string that yields d when read in
 3490|       |			and rounded to nearest.
 3491|       |		1 ==> like 0, but with Steele & White stopping rule;
 3492|       |			e.g. with IEEE P754 arithmetic , mode 0 gives
 3493|       |			1e23 whereas mode 1 gives 9.999999999999999e22.
 3494|       |		2 ==> max(1,ndigits) significant digits.  This gives a
 3495|       |			return value similar to that of ecvt, except
 3496|       |			that trailing zeros are suppressed.
 3497|       |		3 ==> through ndigits past the decimal point.  This
 3498|       |			gives a return value similar to that from fcvt,
 3499|       |			except that trailing zeros are suppressed, and
 3500|       |			ndigits can be negative.
 3501|       |		4,5 ==> similar to 2 and 3, respectively, but (in
 3502|       |			round-nearest mode) with the tests of mode 0 to
 3503|       |			possibly return a shorter string that rounds to d.
 3504|       |			With IEEE arithmetic and compilation with
 3505|       |			-DHonor_FLT_ROUNDS, modes 4 and 5 behave the same
 3506|       |			as modes 2 and 3 when FLT_ROUNDS != 1.
 3507|       |		6-9 ==> Debugging modes similar to mode - 4:  don't try
 3508|       |			fast floating-point estimate (if applicable).
 3509|       |
 3510|       |		Values of mode other than 0-9 are treated as mode 0.
 3511|       |
 3512|       |		Sufficient space is allocated to the return value
 3513|       |		to hold the suppressed trailing zeros.
 3514|       |	*/
